    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Dubrovnik sits at the southern tip of Croatia, on a narrow strip of coast between the Adriatic and the limestone slopes of Mount Srđ. The Old Town fills a rocky headland, with the wooded island of Lokrum a short boat ride offshore.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "For centuries the city ran itself as the Republic of Ragusa, a merchant state that traded across the Mediterranean until Napoleon's forces ended it in 1808. Most of the Baroque streets date from the rebuilding after the 1667 earthquake. UNESCO listed the Old Town in 1979, and the walls withstood shelling again in 1991 and 1992.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "May, June and late September have warm sea water and fewer cruise passengers than July and August. Walk the walls when they open in the morning, before the heat and the crowds build. Order black risotto with cuttlefish ink or oysters from nearby Ston.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
